  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Light and variable flow across ENC this\nafternoon as the stalled front has for the most part dissipated. \nHave seen isolated to scattered showers develop along the sea \nand sound breezes this afternoon. Have yet to see much in the \nway of thunderstorms with gradual increasing heights aloft, \nlack of shear and only modest instability with MLCAPES around \n1000-1500 J/Kg, but could see isolated storms through peak \nheating but will remain sub-severe. PW values remain high, \naround 2.2\" per 12z KMHX sounding, and given slow storm motions,\nisolated areas may pick up an inch or so of rainfall, but most \nareas will see much less with a majority of the FA receiving no\nrain. Expect showers and storms to quickly dissipate early this\nevening with loss of daytime heating. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Ridging builds aloft early this week and peaks\nover the area Wednesday and Thursday with H85 temps around\n21-22C leading to temps well in the 90s most areas away from the\nimmediate coast. Heat headlines may be needed Wednesday,\nThursday, and possibly lingering into Friday with forecast heat\nindices climbing above 105 each day, especially inland areas and\nalong the coast south of US70. Some locations may approach\nrecord high temperature values mid week and have included the\ncurrent record values in the CLIMATE section below. \n\nSubsidence aloft will limit thunderstorm activity for much of\nthe weak with only isolated afternoon convection along the sea\nbreeze at best, but most areas are expected to remain dry\nthrough Thursday. The ridge begins to retreat westward late in \nthe week which NW flow developing aloft bringing the potential \nfor MCV's diving from the Mid-West across the area, possibly as \nearly as Thursday night but more likely late Friday into the\nweekend.",
